---
import { CATALOG } from '../../lab/catalog';
import Lab from '../../layouts/Lab.astro';
---

<Lab title="Tests" description="Scenes that show how pixi-marrow plays on the device that opens them.">
    <main class="lab-index">
        <a href="/" class="lab-back">pixi-marrow</a>
        <h1 class="lab-title">Lab</h1>
        <p class="lab-question">
            Each test plays a scene on the device that opens it, a phone above all, with the settings a game gets when it sets none. Open one and
            keep the screen on. It shows its numbers while it plays, and its result at the end with a text to copy.
        </p>
        <ul class="lab-tests">
            {
                CATALOG.map((test) => (
                    <li>
                        <a href={`/lab/${test.name}/`} class="lab-test">
                            <strong>{test.title}</strong>
                            <span>{test.question}</span>
                            <span class="lab-scene">{test.scene(test.count)}</span>
                        </a>
                    </li>
                ))
            }
        </ul>
        <h2 class="lab-subtitle">Your own skeletons</h2>
        <p class="lab-question">
            The <a href="/playground/" class="lab-link">playground</a> plays any Spine files you drop on it, a whole folder at once, with the numbers of
            every skeleton.
        </p>
        <h2 class="lab-subtitle">Settings in the address</h2>
        <ul class="lab-settings">
            <li><code>?renderer=webgpu</code> draws with WebGPU where the browser has it. WebGL2 is the default.</li>
            <li><code>?n=100</code> sets the number of skeletons.</li>
            <li><code>?seconds=30</code> plays the scene for 30 seconds. The default is 10, and 12 for the slot grid.</li>
        </ul>
        <h2 class="lab-subtitle">How it measures</h2>
        <p class="lab-question">
            A scene plays at the rate of the screen, as in a game, after two seconds to warm up. The lab counts the frames the screen got and the
            ones it dropped, and times the work of the CPU in each frame: the update of every skeleton and the render call of Pixi. Nothing waits
            for the GPU on purpose, so a GPU that cannot keep up shows as dropped frames. How many adds symbols every few seconds while the frames
            keep the rate of the screen.
        </p>
    </main>
</Lab>
